;; Here's an example of how Go 1.17 writes out inlined functions, compiled for
;; linux/amd64. The disassembly of main.main shows two levels of inlining: main
;; calls b, b calls a, a does some work.
;;
;;   inline.go:9   0x4553ec  90              NOPL                 // func main()    { b(v) }
;;   inline.go:6   0x4553ed  90              NOPL                 // func b(v *int) { a(v) }
;;   inline.go:5   0x4553ee  48c7002a000000  MOVQ $0x2a, 0(AX)    // func a(v *int) { *v = 42 }
;;
;; If a profiling signal arrives while executing the MOVQ at 0x4553ee (for line
;; 5), the runtime will report the stack as the MOVQ frame being called by the
;; NOPL at 0x4553ed (for line 6) being called by the NOPL at 0x4553ec (for line
;; 9).
;;
;; The role of pcDeck is to collapse those three frames back into a single
;; location at 0x4553ee, with file/line/function symbolization info representing
;; the three layers of calls. It does that via sequential calls to pcDeck.tryAdd
;; starting with the leaf-most address. The fourth call to pcDeck.tryAdd will be
;; for the caller of main.main. Because main.main was not inlined in its caller,
;; the deck will reject the addition, and the fourth PC on the stack will get
;; its own location.






















(go/type pcDeck "pcDeck is a helper to detect a sequence of inlined functions from\na stack trace returned by the runtime.\n\nThe stack traces returned by runtime's trackback functions are fully\nexpanded (at least for Go functions) and include the fake pcs representing\ninlined functions. The profile proto expects the inlined functions to be\nencoded in one Location message.\nhttps://github.com/google/pprof/blob/5e965273ee43930341d897407202dd5e10e952cb/proto/profile.proto#L177-L184\n\nRuntime does not directly expose whether a frame is for an inlined function\nand looking up debug info is not ideal, so we use a heuristic to filter\nthe fake pcs and restore the inlined and entry functions. Inlined functions\nhave the following properties:\n\n\tFrame's Func is nil (note: also true for non-Go functions), and\n\tFrame's Entry matches its entry function frame's Entry (note: could also be true for recursive calls and non-Go functions), and\n\tFrame's Name does not match its entry function frame's name (note: inlined functions cannot be directly recursive).\n\nAs reading and processing the pcs in a stack trace one by one (from leaf to the root),\nwe use pcDeck to temporarily hold the observed pcs and their expanded frames\nuntil we observe the entry function frame.\n" (struct
    ^{:line 532 :tag (slice uintptr)} pcs
    ^{:line 533 :tag (slice runtime/Frame)} frames
    ^{:line 534 :tag symbolizeFlag} symbolizeResult



    ^{:line 538 :tag int :doc "firstPCFrames indicates the number of frames associated with the first\n(leaf-most) PC in the deck\n"} firstPCFrames


    ^{:line 541 :tag symbolizeFlag :doc "firstPCSymbolizeResult holds the results of the allFrames call for the\nfirst (leaf-most) PC in the deck\n"} firstPCSymbolizeResult))


